		<!-- Comprehensive SEO Guide & Etiquette Rules -->
		<article class="hand-card" style="padding: clamp(1.5rem, 4vw, 3.5rem); margin-bottom: 4rem;">
			
			<div style="text-align: center; max-width: 800px; margin: 0 auto 2.5rem;">
				<h2 class="font-heading" style="font-size: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 2.8rem); color: var(--fg-pencil); margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">
					The Complete Guide to Wedding Envelope Addressing Etiquette
				</h2>
				<p class="font-body" style="font-size: 1.15rem; color: var(--text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">
					Your wedding invitation envelope is the very first tactile impression guests receive. Master envelope sizing, title hierarchies, and postal stamp rules with zero guesswork.
				</p>
			</div>

			<!-- Section 1: Standard Dimensions -->
			<section style="margin-bottom: 3rem;">
				<h3 class="font-heading" style="font-size: 1.75rem; color: var(--accent-red); margin-bottom: 1rem; border-bottom: 2px dashed var(--fg-pencil); padding-bottom: 0.5rem;">
					1. Understanding Envelope Sizes: A7 vs A2 vs Inner Envelopes
				</h3>
				<p class="font-body" style="font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--fg-pencil); margin-bottom: 1rem;">
					In wedding stationery, envelope dimensions use standard letter designations. Selecting the wrong envelope size can lead to cramped inserts or invitations that buckle inside:
				</p>
				<ul class="font-body" style="font-size: 1.02rem; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--fg-pencil); margin-left: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
					<li><strong>A7 Outer Envelope (5.25" × 7.25" / 133mm × 184mm):</strong> The universal standard envelope for 5" × 7" wedding cards. Leaves a clean 1/8" buffer on all sides to accommodate cardstock thickness, pocketfolds, wax seals, and multiple enclosure cards.</li>
					<li><strong>A2 RSVP Reply Envelope (4.375" × 5.75" / 111mm × 146mm):</strong> Holds standard 3.5" × 5" or 4.25" × 5.5" response cards. Pre-addressed to the wedding hosts and pre-stamped with a USPS Forever Stamp.</li>
					<li><strong>A7 Inner Envelope (5.125" × 7" / 130mm × 178mm):</strong> Slips inside the A7 outer envelope unsealed. Specifically names each invited guest (including plus-ones and children) to remove ambiguity about who is invited.</li>
				</ul>
			</section>

			<!-- Section 2: Etiquette Hierarchies -->
			<section style="margin-bottom: 3rem;">
				<h3 class="font-heading" style="font-size: 1.75rem; color: var(--accent-blue); margin-bottom: 1rem; border-bottom: 2px dashed var(--fg-pencil); padding-bottom: 0.5rem;">
					2. Honorifics, Titles & Name Hierarchies
				</h3>
				<p class="font-body" style="font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--fg-pencil); margin-bottom: 1rem;">
					Traditional social etiquette establishes strict protocols for addressing distinguished guests and blended households:
				</p>
				<ul class="font-body" style="font-size: 1.02rem; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--fg-pencil); margin-left: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
					<li><strong>Medical Doctors:</strong> Spell out <em>Doctor</em> on outer envelopes (e.g. <em>Doctor Katherine Evans and Mr. Robert Evans</em>). Because a professional degree outranks a social title, the doctor's name always takes precedence regardless of gender.</li>
					<li><strong>Military Officers & Judges:</strong> Use full rank or title (e.g. <em>Captain James Miller, USN and Mrs. Miller</em> or <em>The Honorable David Vance and Mrs. Vance</em>).</li>
					<li><strong>Widows & Divorced Women:</strong> Traditionally addressed as <em>Mrs. John Smith</em> (for a widow) or <em>Ms. Sarah Jenkins</em> (for a divorced woman using her maiden name).</li>
				</ul>
			</section>

			<!-- Section 3: USPS Postal Rules -->
			<section style="margin-bottom: 3rem;">
				<h3 class="font-heading" style="font-size: 1.75rem; color: var(--fg-pencil); margin-bottom: 1rem; border-bottom: 2px dashed var(--fg-pencil); padding-bottom: 0.5rem;">
					3. USPS Postage, Weight & Wax Seal Rules
				</h3>
				<p class="font-body" style="font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--fg-pencil); margin-bottom: 1rem;">
					Before mailing 100+ invitations, always take one fully assembled suite (with wax seal, belly band, and all inserts) to your local post office window to be hand-weighed:
				</p>
				<div style="background: #f8fafc; border: 1.5px solid var(--fg-pencil); border-radius: 6px; padding: 1.25rem; font-size: 0.98rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--fg-pencil);">
					<strong>🚨 Important Postal Traps to Avoid:</strong><br />
					• <strong>1 oz Weight Limit:</strong> Suites weighing under 1.0 oz require 1 standard 1-ounce Forever Stamp. Suites between 1.01 oz and 2.0 oz require a 2-ounce stamp.<br />
					• <strong>Non-Machinable Surcharge:</strong> Wax seals, ribbon knots, rigid acrylic cards, and square envelopes cannot pass through automated sorting rollers and require a <strong>Non-Machinable surcharge stamp</strong>.<br />
					• <strong>Hand-Canceling:</strong> Request "Hand Canceling" at the post office so a postal clerk stamps the postage by hand rather than feeding delicate calligraphed envelopes through heavy machinery.
				</div>
			</section>

			<!-- Section 4: FAQs -->
			<section>
				<h3 class="font-heading" style="font-size: 1.75rem; color: var(--fg-pencil); margin-bottom: 1.5rem; text-align: center;">
					Frequently Asked Questions
				</h3>

				<details style="margin-bottom: 0.75rem; cursor: pointer;">
					<summary style="font-weight: 700; color: var(--fg-pencil); font-size: 1.05rem;">What is the standard size for a wedding invitation envelope?</summary>
					<p style="font-size: 0.98rem; color: var(--text-secondary); margin-top: 0.5rem; line-height: 1.6;">
						The standard size is an A7 envelope measuring 5.25" × 7.25" (133mm × 184mm), designed to fit a standard 5" × 7" invitation card with enclosure inserts.
					</p>
				</details>

				<details style="margin-bottom: 0.75rem; cursor: pointer;">
					<summary style="font-weight: 700; color: var(--fg-pencil); font-size: 1.05rem;">How do you address a wedding envelope to an unmarried couple living together?</summary>
					<p style="font-size: 0.98rem; color: var(--text-secondary); margin-top: 0.5rem; line-height: 1.6;">
						Stack their names on two separate lines in alphabetical order by last name (e.g. Line 1: Ms. Jessica Taylor, Line 2: Mr. David Miller), followed by their shared street address.
					</p>
				</details>

				<details style="margin-bottom: 0.75rem; cursor: pointer;">
					<summary style="font-weight: 700; color: var(--fg-pencil); font-size: 1.05rem;">How do you address an envelope with a plus-one?</summary>
					<p style="font-size: 0.98rem; color: var(--text-secondary); margin-top: 0.5rem; line-height: 1.6;">
						If the plus-one's name is known, write both names (e.g. <em>Mr. Alexander Clark and Ms. Chloe Vance</em>). If unknown, write <em>Mr. Alexander Clark and Guest</em>.
					</p>
				</details>

				<details style="margin-bottom: 0.75rem; cursor: pointer;">
					<summary style="font-weight: 700; color: var(--fg-pencil); font-size: 1.05rem;">Where does the return address go on wedding envelopes?</summary>
					<p style="font-size: 0.98rem; color: var(--text-secondary); margin-top: 0.5rem; line-height: 1.6;">
						Traditionally, the return address is printed centered on the back flap of the outer envelope. For RSVP envelopes, it is printed centered on the front.
					</p>
				</details>
			</section>

		</article>

